I think people have always been really  interested in how birds fly and  particularly birds are flying a  v-formation  and it's simply because you look up to  the sky and you see these birds and this  such an organized state that you  instantly go how are they doing that why  they're doing it  there's been so many theories put  forward so far to try and explain why  birds flying these distinctive V  formations and these theories range from  a traditional the more birds the better  the predator comes along picks one off  it's not going to be me there's an idea  that the birds at the front might be  better navigators and another one that's  really persisted as this idea of as an  energetic benefit to flying in a V  formation that for those birds flying at  the back they're not having to work as  hard during the flight  the main study so far that shows that  birds can get an energetic benefit from  flying in a V formation as a classic  nature paper for about ten years ago and  they're looking at pelicans flying  across a lake and what they found was  the bird at the front had a much higher  heart rate than the rest of the birds in  the V and they were using heart rate as  a proxy for energy expenditure so that  was the first real empirical evidence  that there might be some truth to this  sort of long-standing idea as an  energetic benefit to being in a  v-formation we were lucky enough to work  with a conservation group based in  Austria who are trying to reintroduce  this critically endangered bird called  northern bull divers back into his  former range in the Alps and they do  that by hatching the eggs onto human  foster parents and the foster parents  then spend all spring and summer with  these birds who become habituated and  imprinted on them and then they have  these birds following a microlight  containing these foster parents who are  constantly calling out to the birds  follow us follow us and the birds follow  this microlight and they learn what  should be their correct migration route  to their wintering grounds  so far it's been very difficult to study  flight in free flying birds because if  you start to put equipment on them and  then they fly off into the distance the  chances are you're not going to see that  equipment again you need to get that  equipment back to get the information  off it for many years we've been working  towards creating these loggers are able  to measure where an animal is and what  it's doing there so whether it was  flapping how hard was it flapping how  fast was it going when you start to put  all this together you can really start  to investigate the mechanisms and the  interactions that are going on between  individuals within the flock so these  are the loggers that we were developing  in our lab that went on the birds you  can see here they have quite a large  battery which is essential for the quite  long sampling periods this antenna here  receives the GPS information it doesn't  emit it and that's what made this such a  good collaboration because these birds  would been flown and then returning home  at the end of the day so if you get the  logger off and get the data off it to  the bird start of training for that  migration around June July and they have  these little local training flights  whether it's learning how to fly how to  fly as a group and also to make sure  they follow the microlight  and then towards the end of the summer  kind of late August the actual full  migration begins and this migration  takes them from Salzburg in Austria  right round to southern Italy but to  avoid the Alps which might be a little  bit problematic for the young bird we  take a slightly convoluted journey  through Austria right round flew  souvenir and then down into Italy when  the birds take off it takes them around  four to five minutes to sort of  orientate themselves into this  traditional v-shape for they kind of  start heading off  the first really interesting result that  we got off the loggers was that the  birds were locating themselves in  positions within the V formation that  was completely predicted by fixed-wing  aerodynamics and what we mean by that is  they were positioning themselves in the  best possible spot to take advantage of  the good air the upwash coming off the  bird in front so the aromatics behind  yov might make sense goes like this if  you have a bird flapping along it has to  throw air down in order for it to stay  up this means that instead of the air  building up at the bottom or on the  ground  it squeezes around the edge at which  point you've got the circulation of air  that looks like this so it got downwash  here but upwash here which means the  bird flapping along here could fly in  effectively upwards moving air and so it  doesn't have a flaps are harder to stay  on and then if you add that another bird  then you get a half a v-formation to it  both sides and got a v-formation tagging  up so even though the birds fitted  fixed-wing aerodynamics where they  position themselves we can't ignore the  fact they were flapping and in fact this  is where the most interesting result was  and what they're doing with their  flapping and when they're flapping along  and what we totally weren't expecting  was that they might be paying attention  to the flat phasing of the bird ahead so  if you're flapping then this this up was  the river this whirl of air behind you  is going up and down up and down with  your wings but both of the really neat  thing is we could show that if this one  is a whole wavelength ahead then this  one flaps in phase so that that wingtip  path which is this winter  and if it's half a wavelength behind  then they flat like that which means  that wingtip path is still matching that  way to path  the implications of this research could  be quite far-reaching so for example  airlines have been investing heavily to  try and understand how birds can get so  close together to take advantage of as  upwash 